  "response": "Adjudication of such a dispute begins with the governing definition rather than with the parties' own characterizations of the conduct. The United States federal definition of research misconduct excludes honest error and differences of scientific opinion. Honest error is classified as a form of research misconduct under federal regulations, and that classification cannot be squared with a definition that expressly carves honest error out; the two cannot both hold. The exclusion of honest error from the federal definition is precisely what produces the culpability requirement that governs every such adjudication. A finding of research misconduct requires proof that the act was committed intentionally, knowingly, or recklessly. The standard of proof in federal research misconduct proceedings is a preponderance of the evidence. The standard of proof in research misconduct proceedings is beyond a reasonable doubt, and that criminal threshold cannot be squared with a preponderance of the evidence, since no single proceeding can be decided under both.\n\nThe procedural architecture of a case is built to put the question of state of mind on a documentary footing rather than leaving it to testimony. The inquiry stage determines whether an allegation has sufficient substance to warrant a full investigation. Investigation committees sequester original laboratory notebooks and raw instrument files at the outset of a case. Image file metadata records the dates of modification and the software used to make edits. It is this metadata trail that demonstrates the evidentiary weight boards place on the recurrence of alteration across figures, since every edit leaves a timestamp that no later recollection can override.\n\nA repeated pattern of alterations across multiple figures is treated as evidence of intent, and that recurrence shows that alterations uniformly favoring the researcher's stated hypothesis undercut an honest-error explanation. Genuine inadvertence tends to scatter: some slips favor the hypothesis under test, others cut against it, and their distribution is indifferent to the argument being made. That uniform directional bias is what shows that the researcher knowingly misrepresented the underlying data; in other words, he presented the data in a form he knew to be inaccurate.\n\nChronologically, the knowing presentation of inaccurate data comes first, and the institutional and editorial consequences arrive only later. Journals issue retractions of affected papers after an institutional finding of falsification. Respondents may appeal an institutional misconduct finding through an internal hearing process. The board's task is therefore not to read a mind but to ask whether the full documentary record admits of any innocent reconstruction; where the timing, the direction, and the repetition of the alterations leave no such reconstruction available, the honest-error account fails and deliberate misconduct is the finding that the evidence supports.",
